    {
      "answer": "4",
      "reason_rejected": "This answer divides the total deficit ($300,000) by the gross new salary ($75,000). This is logically flawed because it assumes the entire new salary is 'extra' money. In reality, $25,000 of that salary is merely the baseline he would have earned anyway. The 'money equivalent' to the lost wages and loans is the *incremental* wealth generated by the degree. If he uses his full $75,000 salary to pay off the $300,000, he is effectively using the $25,000 baseline to pay off the $100,000 lost wages, which double-counts the opportunity cost (he didn't lose the $25k baseline, he just didn't receive it yet; but using it to pay off the 'lost' $25k implies he gets it back twice or ignores that the baseline is not a gain). The correct metric for 'earning back' the cost of the investment is the net gain (incremental income)."
    },
    {
      "answer": "10",
      "reason_rejected": "This answer adds the 4 years of college to the 6 years of post-college earning. The question asks 'how long would it take to earn the money equivalent', which refers to the duration of the earning period required to recoup the costs, not the total elapsed time from the start of the decision. The 4 years of college are the period where the debt was incurred and income lost, not the period where it is being earned back."
